// ============================================================================
// Hunt: LiteLLM 1.107.0 supply-chain compromise — trojaned PyPI/Docker
//   release shipped through a hijacked release pipeline (Oct 21, 2026)
// Context: On Oct 21, 2026, attackers used stolen GitHub CLI OAuth tokens
//   from three LiteLLM engineers' accounts to run the project's release
//   workflow against a lookalike repository (Berri-AI/litellm, one hyphen
//   away from the legitimate fork) and publish version 1.107.0. One token
//   launched the pipeline, a second approved it, a third deleted the
//   workflow records. LiteLLM believes the tokens were lifted from logs
//   in an internet-exposed internal deployment. All seven PyPI wheels
//   were live 11:39-13:23 UTC; Docker Hub and GHCR tags (v1.107.0,
//   latest, main-latest, main-stable) served the tainted build from
//   11:46 UTC, with GHCR pulled by 13:51 UTC and Docker Hub repointed by
//   14:20 UTC. The malicious build steals credentials — cloud logins,
//   developer secrets, AI service keys, browser passwords, and LiteLLM
//   proxy database contents — and loads remote-code execution tooling.
//   Wiz researchers report stolen AWS credentials were tested against
//   Amazon Bedrock within hours. Last safe version is 1.106.2; anyone
//   who installed 1.107.0 or pulled those tags on Oct 21 should treat
//   the host as compromised, rebuild from a clean image, and rotate
//   every secret the process could read. This is LiteLLM's second
//   supply-chain compromise of 2026 (1.82.7/1.82.8 were the first).
// MITRE ATT&CK: T1195.002 (Supply Chain Compromise: Compromise Software
//               Supply Chain), T1078 (Valid Accounts - stolen GitHub
//               OAuth tokens), T1555.003 (Credentials from Web Browsers)
// Data: Microsoft Defender - DeviceProcessEvents, DeviceFileEvents
// Write-up: https://amitvijayan.com/journal/articles/daily-cyber-threat-brief-october-24-2026.html
// Logic: version 1.107.0 is trojaned wherever it appears, so any pip
//   install naming litellm 1.107.0 is a hit, not a version-drift alert.
//   The floating Docker tags (latest, main-latest, main-stable) are only
//   incriminating inside the Oct 21 taint window, so that variant is
//   time-boxed. The post-install variant hunts the stealer's own
//   behavior: a Python/LiteLLM process reading browser credential stores
//   or spawning a shell.
// ============================================================================

// -- Variant 1: pip install of trojaned litellm 1.107.0
// Any host that installed litellm==1.107.0 picked up the credential
// stealer. Match on the installer (pip, pip3, uv, python -m pip) plus
// both the package name and the exact trojaned version in the command.
DeviceProcessEvents
| where TimeGenerated > ago(30d)
| where FileName in~ ("pip.exe", "pip3.exe", "uv.exe", "python.exe", "pythonw.exe", "py.exe")
| where ProcessCommandLine has "litellm" and ProcessCommandLine has "1.107.0"
| project TimeGenerated, DeviceName, FileName, ProcessCommandLine,
    InitiatingProcessFileName, InitiatingProcessCommandLine, AccountName
| order by TimeGenerated desc

// -- Variant 2: Docker pull/run of tainted LiteLLM tags in the Oct 21 window
// v1.107.0 is always trojaned; the floating tags (latest, main-latest,
// main-stable) served the tainted build only 11:46-14:20 UTC on Oct 21,
// so floating-tag matches are time-boxed to that window to avoid flagging
// legitimate later pulls of the same tag names.
DeviceProcessEvents
| where FileName =~ "docker.exe"
| where ProcessCommandLine has "litellm"
| extend IsPinnedBadVersion = ProcessCommandLine has "1.107.0",
         IsFloatingTagInWindow = (ProcessCommandLine has_any ("latest", "main-latest", "main-stable")
             and TimeGenerated between (datetime(2026-10-21T11:46:00Z) .. datetime(2026-10-21T14:20:00Z)))
| where IsPinnedBadVersion or IsFloatingTagInWindow
| project TimeGenerated, DeviceName, ProcessCommandLine,
    InitiatingProcessFileName, AccountName, IsPinnedBadVersion, IsFloatingTagInWindow
| order by TimeGenerated desc

// -- Variant 3a: Python/LiteLLM process reading Chromium "Login Data"
// The 1.107.0 payload steals browser passwords. Only the browser itself
// should touch Login Data; a litellm-invoking Python process doing so is
// the stealer executing.
DeviceFileEvents
| where TimeGenerated > ago(30d)
| where FileName =~ "Login Data"
| where InitiatingProcessFileName in~ ("python.exe", "pythonw.exe")
| where InitiatingProcessCommandLine has "litellm"
| project TimeGenerated, DeviceName, FolderPath, FileName,
    InitiatingProcessFileName, InitiatingProcessCommandLine, InitiatingProcessAccountName
| order by TimeGenerated desc

// -- Variant 3b: shell spawned by a litellm-invoking Python process
// The payload also loads remote-code execution tooling. A shell child of
// a Python process that was invoked for litellm is that tooling running.
DeviceProcessEvents
| where TimeGenerated > ago(30d)
| where FileName in~ ("cmd.exe", "powershell.exe", "pwsh.exe", "sh", "bash")
| where InitiatingProcessFileName in~ ("python.exe", "pythonw.exe")
| where InitiatingProcessCommandLine has "litellm"
| project TimeGenerated, DeviceName, FileName, ProcessCommandLine,
    InitiatingProcessFileName, InitiatingProcessCommandLine, AccountName
| order by TimeGenerated desc
